      "sensorHeightMm": 24.0,
      "clipStartM": 3.107125103623776,
      "clipEndM": 4.118946968135317,
      "derivation": "The framing rule of board:0083 (src/gltf/Framing.h) applied to this subject's own bounds and to nothing else: azimuth 35 deg and elevation 20 deg in glTF's +Y-up frame, distance r / sin(yfov/2) / 0.6 at r = 0.50591093225577066 m about the centre (0, 0.084497897059367943, 0), yfov = 2*atan(12/50) from Blender's factory 50 mm lens on a 24 mm sensor height at sensor_fit VERTICAL, near = distance - r and far = distance + r. THE ROLL IS ZERO AND STATED: this subject's silhouette is not rational at any roll, so a roll would be a different draw of the same lottery rather than an exactness argument. ",
      "note": "The file carries no camera, so this case's camera is declared here and the oracle builds the same one from these numbers."
    },
    "light": {
      "kind": "none",
      "note": "The only illumination is Blender's factory world, and it is left where it is because configuring it would be a second thing to keep honest."
    },
    "world": {
      "kind": "factory",
      "note": "Configured by not being configured. The observed colour and strength go into provenance.json, so a Blender that changed them is visible without any machinery that refuses."
    },
    "material": {
      "source": "gltf-base-colour",
      "carriedBy": "texture",
      "kind": "emission",
      "note": "The file's own base-colour images and uv sets through an emitter. A chess set on a board is the clearest case there is of surfaces that see one another -- every piece shades the board and its neighbours -- so a Diffuse BSDF at one sample per pixel would score an ambient-occlusion estimator (board:0087). The lowering follows the link into each Principled BSDF's Base Color, so the 22 normal and ORM maps are left unreached rather than picked up by position."
    }
  },
